+- **Frame generation.** `build_wine_ps5.sh --framegen DIR`, with DIR a
+  checkout of ps5-framegen at the commit `tools/link_radv_prx.sh` pins, links
+  its interpolator (AMD FSR3's optical flow and frame interpolation as
+  compute shaders, embedded as SPIR-V) into RADV's `libvulkan.prx`, with
+  `wine/ps5/pw_framegen.cpp`; without it `wine/ps5/pw_framegen_none.c`
+  passes every entry point through. The interpolator calls Vulkan through
+  a table filled from RADV's own entry points (`wine/ps5/pw_framegen_vk.h`),
+  since Mesa's archive exports none. With `PW_PC5_FRAMEGEN=1` in the
+  environment (PC5 sets it for a game whose Frame generation is on), the
+  resolver wraps the device, its queues, its swapchains and their presents:
+  a swapchain gets one image more, and transfer use, for the frame in
+  between; the game's presents are held to one each 1/29.92 s, half the
+  display's 59.94 Hz less a little so the hold never waits a whole refresh;
+  and each present copies the game's image to the interpolator, makes the
+  frame between it and the one before, and presents that frame, then the
+  game's. It engages after 8 frames on time and stops at a frame more than
+  a quarter of a period late, showing the game's frames as they come until
+  it keeps up again; a frame after a pause is not interpolated across. A
+  swapchain whose format is not 8-bit RGB, or for which the interpolator
+  cannot be made, presents as it is. Measured on the PC with lavapipe (the
+  same glue against Mesa's software driver, headless, with the validation
+  layers): every frame presented, the frames in between generated and no
+  validation errors; ps5-framegen's own checks of its interpolator (moving
+  scenes against their known midpoints) pass. ps5-framegen is
+  GPL-3.0-or-later and its shaders MIT, so a `libvulkan.prx` linked with it
+  is GPL-3.0-or-later.
